  <!-- AEO Top Answer Summary Box -->
  <section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-label="Quick Answer Summary">
    <div class="info-callout speakable-summary">
      <h3>Quick Summary: How Time Zones Work</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Time zones work by dividing the Earth's 360-degree sphere into 24 longitudinal bands of 15 degrees each, corresponding to one hour of Earth's axial rotation.</strong> 
        Because Earth rotates from west to east once every 24 hours (360° / 24 hours = 15° per hour), every 15-degree movement east advances local solar time by 1 hour, while moving west subtracts 1 hour. 
        All global time zones are measured as positive or negative offsets from <a href="/what-is-utc/">Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)</a>, anchored at the Prime Meridian (0° longitude) in Greenwich, London. 
        Civil governments adjust these theoretical 15-degree lines around national borders, creating regional zones like <a href="/blog/what-is-est/">Eastern Standard Time (EST, UTC-5)</a>, <a href="/what-is-edt/">Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4)</a>, and fractional zones like India (UTC+5:30).
      </p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="reading-meta" style="margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
      <span>⏱️ Estimated Reading Time: <strong>16 minutes</strong> | 🌐 Subject: <strong>Astrophysics, Longitude &amp; Software DB Architecture</strong></span>
    </div>
  </section>

