  <!-- SECTION 1 -->
  <section id="earth-rotation-math" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Earth Rotation Physics and Math">
    <h2>1. The Physics of Earth Rotation &amp; 15-Degree Math</h2>
    <p>
      The concept of time zones is rooted in fundamental celestial mechanics. Earth is a spheroid measuring 360 degrees in circumference. It completes one full revolution around its axis relative to the Sun in approximately 24 hours (a solar day).
    </p>
    <p>
      Dividing the full rotation by the hours in a day yields the mathematical constant governing global timekeeping:
    </p>
    <p>
      {"$$\\frac{360^\\circ \\text{ Longitude}}{24 \\text{ Hours}} = 15^\\circ \\text{ Longitude per Hour}$$"}
    </p>
    <p>
      This means that for every 15 degrees of longitude you travel eastward, the Sun crosses the local meridian exactly 1 hour earlier. Conversely, for every 15 degrees you travel westward, solar noon occurs 1 hour later.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, <a href="/new-york-time/">New York City</a> sits at approximately 74° West longitude. Dividing 74° by 15° per hour equals roughly 4.9 hours west of the Greenwich meridian, placing the US East Coast squarely in the <a href="/blog/what-is-est/">UTC-5 (EST)</a> time zone. Learn more in <a href="/why-time-zones-exist/">Why Time Zones Exist</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SVG Globe Diagram -->
  <section id="svg-globe-diagram" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Visual Diagram Longitudinal Band Division">
    <h2>2. Visualizing Earth's 24 Longitudinal Time Bands</h2>
    <p>
      The diagram below illustrates how Earth's 360-degree surface is partitioned into 24 standard hourly bands centered on the Prime Meridian:
    </p>

    <div class="infographic-wrapper">
      <svg class="visual-svg" viewBox="0 0 800 340" width="100%" height="auto" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Earth 24 Longitudinal Bands Diagram">
        <rect width="800" height="340" rx="12" fill="#0f172a" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2"/>
        
        <!-- Center Prime Meridian -->
        <line x1="400" y1="40" x2="400" y2="260" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="3"/>
        <text x="400" y="30" fill="#38bdf8" font-family="Space Grotesk, sans-serif" font-weight="700" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle">PRIME MERIDIAN (0°)</text>
        <text x="400" y="280" fill="#38bdf8" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">UTC+0 / GMT (London)</text>

        <!-- West Bands -->
        <line x1="280" y1="40" x2="280" y2="260" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4"/>
        <text x="280" y="280" fill="#60a5fa" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">-75° W: UTC-5 (EST)</text>

        <line x1="160" y1="40" x2="160" y2="260" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4"/>
        <text x="160" y="280" fill="#60a5fa" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">-120° W: UTC-8 (PST)</text>

        <line x1="50" y1="40" x2="50" y2="260" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4"/>
        <text x="50" y="280" fill="#60a5fa" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">-180°: IDL (West)</text>

        <!-- East Bands -->
        <line x1="520" y1="40" x2="520" y2="260" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4"/>
        <text x="520" y="280" fill="#34d399" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">+82.5° E: UTC+5:30 (IST)</text>

        <line x1="640" y1="40" x2="640" y2="260" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4"/>
        <text x="640" y="280" fill="#34d399" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">+135° E: UTC+9 (JST)</text>

        <line x1="750" y1="40" x2="750" y2="260" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4"/>
        <text x="750" y="280" fill="#34d399" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">+180°: IDL (East)</text>

        <!-- Rotation Arrow -->
        <path d="M 200 150 C 350 100 450 100 600 150" fill="none" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow-rot)"/>
        <text x="400" y="115" fill="#fbbf24" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Earth Rotates West to East (15° / Hour)</text>

        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow-rot" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="5" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#fbbf24" />
