        </defs>
      </svg>
      <p class="infographic-caption">Figure 1: Earth's 360-Degree Longitudinal Band Division relative to the Prime Meridian (0°).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="solar-noon-vs-civil" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Solar Noon vs Civil Clock Time">
    <h2>3. Solar Noon vs Civil Clock Time</h2>
    <p>
      Before the 19th century, every town operated on local solar time. When the sun reached its highest point in the local sky, town clocks were set to 12:00 PM. 
    </p>
    <p>
      However, because Earth moves in an elliptical orbit and sits at a 23.5-degree axial tilt, solar noon fluctuates by up to 16 minutes throughout the year (a phenomenon calculated by the <em>Equation of Time</em>). Furthermore, neighboring towns just 15 miles apart had clocks differing by 1 full minute.
    </p>
    <p>
      Standard time zones solved this confusion by grouping 15-degree slices of Earth under a single <strong>civil clock time</strong>. While solar noon in eastern Maine occurs at 11:42 AM civil clock time and solar noon in western Michigan occurs at 12:35 PM, both regions operate comfortably within the broader Eastern Time zone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="fractional-offsets" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Non Standard Fractional Offsets">
    <h2>4. Non-Standard Fractional Offsets (+5:30, +9:30, +4:45)</h2>
    <p>
      While most time zones use full 1-hour increments relative to UTC, several nations use half-hour or 45-minute fractional offsets to align their civil clocks more accurately with local solar noon:
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Region / Country</th>
            <th>Civil Time Zone Name</th>
            <th>UTC Offset</th>
            <th>Rationale / History</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>India</strong></td>
            <td>India Standard Time (IST)</td>
            <td><strong>UTC+5:30</strong></td>
            <td>Splits the difference between 75° E (Allahabad) and 90° E meridians. Read <a href="/gmt-to-ist/">GMT to IST Guide</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Central Australia</strong></td>
            <td>Australian Central Standard (ACST)</td>
            <td><strong>UTC+9:30</strong></td>
            <td>Centers South Australia and Northern Territory between Perth (UTC+8) and Sydney (UTC+10). See <a href="/australia-time-zones/">Australia Time Zones</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Nepal</strong></td>
            <td>Nepal Time (NPT)</td>
            <td><strong>UTC+5:45</strong></td>
            <td>Anchored precisely to the meridian passing over Mount Gaurishankar (86° 15' E longitude).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Newfoundland (Canada)</strong></td>
            <td>Newfoundland Standard (NST)</td>
            <td><strong>UTC-3:30</strong></td>
            <td>Positioned on North America's easternmost island peninsula. Explore <a href="/canada-time-zones/">Canada Time Zones</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Chatham Islands (NZ)</strong></td>
            <td>Chatham Standard Time (CHAST)</td>
            <td><strong>UTC+12:45</strong></td>
            <td>Remote Pacific archipelago 800 km east of New Zealand's mainland.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="political-boundaries" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Political Borders vs Geographical Longitude">
    <h2>5. Political Borders vs Geographical Longitude</h2>
    <p>
      Time zones are ultimately defined by national legislation rather than physical geography. Political boundaries create striking anomalies across the world map:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>China's 5-Zone Span:</strong> Geographically, China covers 5 full time zones. However, since 1949, the government mandates a single national time zone—Beijing Time (UTC+8)—across the entire 3,000-mile landmass. As a result, in western Xinjiang, the sun does not rise until 10:00 AM in winter.</li>
      <li><strong>Spain &amp; Central Europe:</strong> Spain sits geographically in the same longitudinal band as the United Kingdom and should logically use GMT (UTC+0). However, in 1940, General Franco shifted Spain's clocks to Central European Time (CET, UTC+1) to align with Germany, creating late summer sunsets near 10:00 PM.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <section id="iana-database" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="The IANA Time Zone Database tzdb">
    <h2>6. The IANA Time Zone Database (tzdb) &amp; Software Parsing</h2>
    <p>
      Computer operating systems, smartphones, and global web servers cannot rely on simple numeric offsets because daylight saving rules change frequently. Instead, software relies on the <strong>IANA Time Zone Database</strong> (maintained by Paul Eggert and ICANN community volunteers).
    </p>
    <p>
      The IANA database assigns unique geographic keys in the format <code>Area/Location</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>America/New_York</code> (Handles both EST UTC-5 and EDT UTC-4 automatically).</li>
      <li><code>Europe/London</code> (Handles GMT UTC+0 and BST UTC+1 automatically).</li>
      <li><code>Asia/Kolkata</code> (Handles year-round IST UTC+5:30).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Operating systems parse POSIX rule strings (e.g. <code>EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0</code>) or zoneinfo binary files to convert UTC system timestamps into local display times effortlessly. See <a href="/how-to-convert-time-zones/">How to Convert Time Zones</a> for conversion algorithms.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <section id="international-date-line" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="The International Date Line and Midnight Rollover">
    <h2>7. The International Date Line &amp; Midnight Rollover</h2>
    <p>
      The International Date Line (IDL) sits opposite the Prime Meridian at roughly 180° longitude in the Pacific Ocean. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Crossing the IDL traveling westward (from the US to Asia) advances your calendar date by 1 day (e.g., from Tuesday to Wednesday). Crossing traveling eastward (from Asia to the US) subtracts 1 day (e.g., repeating Tuesday). Read our comprehensive guide on <a href="/international-date-line-explained/">The International Date Line Explained</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 9: TIMEZONE MATRIX -->
  <section id="timezone-matrix" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Global Offset Spectrum Table">
    <h2>8. Global Offset Spectrum Table (-12:00 to +14:00)</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>UTC Offset</th>
            <th>Representative Regions / Cities</th>
            <th>Primary Standard Acronym</th>
